53:Coronary atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major cause of acute coronary events, and its risk is governed jointly by plaque morphology, tissue material properties, and hemodynamic loading. Which of these factor groups most strongly governs rupture-risk variability remains poorly characterized, partly because pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) is computationally expensive. Here, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes, sampled by Latin hypercube design over morphological, material, and hemodynamic parameters. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, the peak plaque structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta$PSS), were evaluated and first checked for directional consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis was performed at the individual-variable and factor-group levels. $\Delta$PSS was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three, indicating that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. The surrogates were accurate ($R^2 = 0.94$--$0.97$). Across both phenotypes and indices, the vessel-wall modulus, fibrous-cap modulus, and systolic pressure were the dominant contributors, and the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas morphology contributed mainly through interactions. These results indicate that coronary plaque rupture-risk variability is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone, providing a basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate patient-specific characterization.
108:For the fluid inlet boundary condition, a physiological pressure waveform $P_\mathrm{in}(t)$ was prescribed. This waveform was generated by coupling a fixed reference coronary flow waveform $Q(t)$ with a three-element Windkessel (RCR) model, fixing the coronary resistance ratio at $R_p/R_d = 1/10$ and fitting the remaining parameters by least squares to the target $(P_\mathrm{sys},\ P_\mathrm{dia} = P_\mathrm{sys} - \Delta P)$~\cite{westerhof2009windkessel}. Thus, $P_\mathrm{in}(t)$ for each sample is fully determined by the three hemodynamic parameters: systolic pressure $P_\mathrm{sys}$, pulse pressure $\Delta P$, and diastolic decay ratio $\tau$ (Table~\ref{tab:inputs}). For the outlet boundary condition, a lumped-parameter coronary model was imposed at each outlet to represent the distal coronary microvasculature, with a time-varying intramyocardial pressure accounting for myocardial compression~\cite{kim2010coronary}. Its parameters were calibrated to reference coronary flow and pressure values~\cite{guyton2011_av_P, Sankaran2012}. For the solid boundary condition, the wall pressure field reconstructed from the steady 3D fluid simulation was applied as a surface traction on the inner wall of the multi-domain solid mesh, and both ends of the segment were constrained as fixed ends. The fluid and solid boundary conditions are summarized in Fig.~\ref{fig:bc}.
187:The main findings of this study can be summarized as follows. First, $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five clinically recognized high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three of the five. This result shows that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. Second, the global sensitivity analysis showed that the variability of both indices was governed predominantly by material properties ($E_\mathrm{vessel}$ and $E_\mathrm{FC}$), with systolic pressure ($P_\mathrm{sys}$) acting as a hemodynamic contributor. As a prerequisite for interpreting these findings, the simulation outputs were compared with previously reported computational and clinical evidence.
