53:Coronary atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major cause of acute coronary events, and its risk is governed jointly by plaque morphology, tissue material properties, and hemodynamic loading. Which of these factor groups most strongly governs rupture-risk variability remains poorly characterized, partly because pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) is computationally expensive. Here, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes, sampled by Latin hypercube design over morphological, material, and hemodynamic parameters. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, the peak plaque structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta$PSS), were evaluated and first checked for directional consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis was performed at the individual-variable and factor-group levels. $\Delta$PSS was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three, indicating that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. The surrogates were accurate ($R^2 = 0.94$--$0.97$). Across both phenotypes and indices, the vessel-wall modulus, fibrous-cap modulus, and systolic pressure were the dominant contributors, and the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas morphology contributed mainly through interactions. These results indicate that coronary plaque rupture-risk variability is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone, providing a basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate patient-specific characterization.
81:Accordingly, this study aimed to determine which of the morphological, material, and hemodynamic factor groups most strongly governs the variability of coronary plaque rupture risk. A cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large computational datasets for low-attenuation and calcified plaque phenotypes. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were evaluated. Their directional consistency with clinically recognized high-risk plaque features was first assessed before sensitivity interpretation. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, followed by Sobol variance-based GSA at both the individual-variable and factor-group levels. This framework quantifies direct and total contributions, including interaction-related effects, across broad physiologically plausible input ranges. The analysis was designed to identify the dominant determinants of plaque-vulnerability variability and to provide a mechanistic basis for prioritizing quantities that warrant accurate characterization in future patient-specific assessment.
134:\subsection{Clinical consistency of the vulnerability indices}
136:This stage examines whether the two vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, behave consistently with clinically recognized markers of plaque vulnerability. Five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features were used for this check. Each feature was represented by a model-measurable quantity, and the expected sign of the correlation between that quantity and each VI was defined according to its association with plaque vulnerability. For example, the high-risk feature ``soft plaque'' was represented by the lipid-core modulus $E_\mathrm{lipid}$, for which a negative correlation with each VI was expected because a softer lipid core is associated with greater plaque vulnerability.
168:\subsection{Clinical consistency of the vulnerability indices}
169:The two vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were checked against the five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features listed in Table~\ref{tab:consistency}. For each feature, the sign of the Spearman correlation between the representative model quantity and each VI was compared with the expected high-risk direction (Fig.~\ref{fig:consistency}).
220:Using this forward solver, large input--output datasets were generated for the low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$, were evaluated for their directional consistency with clinically established high-risk plaque features. $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ was consistent with all five features, whereas PSS was consistent with three of the five. This result indicates that plaque rupture vulnerability is more consistently captured by the pulsatile stress amplitude than by the peak systolic stress alone, supporting the use of pulsatile rather than static analysis.
283:\caption{Clinical high-risk plaque features used to check the clinical consistency of the vulnerability indices.}
287:\# & Clinical high rupture-risk feature & Representative model quantity & Expected correlation sign with VI & Data source \\
302:\caption{Clinical consistency of the two vulnerability indices. Expected correlation direction between each model quantity and the VI, and the consistency of PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$. \checkmark: expected direction satisfied; $\times$: violates expected direction.}
