328:    \caption{Overview of the proposed three-stage framework. \textbf{Step 1:} a snapshot-based FSI solver maps morphological, hemodynamic, and material inputs to the peak structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$), evaluated over the fibrous-cap surface nodes ($\Gamma_{FC}$). \textbf{Step 2:} the two indices are checked for sign-consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features (shown for plaque burden). \textbf{Step 3:} a Gaussian process regression surrogate is trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based sensitivity analysis quantifies each factor group's contribution to rupture-risk variability.}
336:    \caption{Idealized geometric models of the two coronary plaque phenotypes. \textbf{(A)} Anatomical context and plaque classification: a coronary lesion (inset) is modeled as either a low-attenuation plaque (LAP), a three-domain model of vessel wall, fibrous cap, and lipid core, or a calcified plaque (CP), a four-domain model with an additional calcification inside the lipid core. \textbf{(B)} Parametric definition of the lesion geometry, shown for the LAP: (i) the lesion region within the 10-cm cylindrical vessel segment, and (ii) the cross-section at the minimum-lumen-area (MLA) plane, $z = z_\mathrm{MLA}$. The geometric parameters are defined in Table~\ref{tab:inputs}.}
344:    \caption{Boundary conditions for the fluid and solid analyses. The fluid domain (inlet, deformable lumen) and solid domain are shown together with the outlet lumped-parameter coronary/Windkessel circuit. \textbf{(A)} Prescribed physiological inlet pressure waveform $P_\mathrm{in}(t)$, defined by systolic pressure $P_\mathrm{sys}$, pulse pressure $\Delta P$, and decay ratio $\tau$. \textbf{(B)} Representative lumped-parameter circuit values used for the outlet boundary model.}
352:    \caption{Rupture-location classification. \textbf{(A)} Axial classification into proximal, mid, and distal regions using the lumen area ratio $LA(z) = A_\mathrm{lumen}/A_\mathrm{normal}$, with the mid region bounded by the two locations where $LA = 0.3$. \textbf{(B)} Circumferential classification on the rupture plane, where the angle $\theta_\mathrm{rupture}$ is normalized by the lipid arc angle $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$ to define center ($I_\theta < 0.5$) versus shoulder ($I_\theta \geq 0.5$) locations.}
360:    \caption{Simulated stress, FFR, and rupture-location results from the LAP dataset. \textbf{(A)} Distributions of simulated PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$. \textbf{(B, C)} Circumferential (center/shoulder) and axial (proximal/mid/distal) classification of the predicted high-risk locations. \textbf{(D)} Simulated FFR--DoS relationship compared with reported clinical data. \textbf{(E)} PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ versus fibrous-cap thickness ($t_\mathrm{FC}$) from the auxiliary validation set, shown for three fibrous-cap moduli ($E_\mathrm{FC}$).}
368:    \caption{Clinical consistency check of the vulnerability indices. Sign of the correlation between each representative model quantity and each vulnerability index (PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$) for the five high-risk plaque features, evaluated on the LAP and CP datasets, compared with the expected high-risk direction. $PB$: plaque burden; $\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$: functional (FFR) gradient; $E_\mathrm{lipid}$: lipid-core modulus; $\theta_\mathrm{lipid,max}$: maximum lipid arc; $f_\mathrm{calc}$: calcification fraction.}
376:    \caption{Sobol sensitivity analysis (first- and total-order indices) of the selected vulnerability indices. \textbf{(A)} Individual-variable and \textbf{(B)} group-level results for the morphology, material, and hemodynamic groups, shown for PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ in the low-attenuation plaque (LAP) and calcified plaque (CP) datasets.}

