52	\begin{abstract}
53	Coronary atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major cause of acute coronary events, and its risk is governed jointly by plaque morphology, tissue material properties, and hemodynamic loading. Which of these factor groups most strongly governs rupture-risk variability remains poorly characterized, partly because pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) is computationally expensive. Here, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes, sampled by Latin hypercube design over morphological, material, and hemodynamic parameters. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, the peak plaque structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta$PSS), were evaluated and first checked for directional consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis was performed at the individual-variable and factor-group levels. $\Delta$PSS was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three, indicating that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. The surrogates were accurate ($R^2 = 0.94$--$0.97$). Across both phenotypes and indices, the vessel-wall modulus, fibrous-cap modulus, and systolic pressure were the dominant contributors, and the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas morphology contributed mainly through interactions. These results indicate that coronary plaque rupture-risk variability is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone, providing a basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate patient-specific characterization.
54	\end{abstract}
55	
56	\begin{highlights}
57	\item Morphological, material, and hemodynamic factors varied jointly in a large FSI set
58	\item Peak (PSS) and pulsatile ($\Delta$PSS) plaque structural stress compared as risk indices
59	\item Pulsatile $\Delta$PSS is more clinically consistent than peak systolic stress (PSS)
60	\item Sobol sensitivity analysis ranks material properties as the top rupture-risk factor
61	\end{highlights}
62	
63	\begin{keyword}
64	Coronary plaque rupture \sep Plaque structural stress \sep Global sensitivity analysis \sep Sobol indices \sep Fluid--structure interaction \sep Gaussian process regression \sep Vulnerability index
65	\end{keyword}
66	
67	\end{frontmatter}
68	
69	%% ============================================================
70	\section{Introduction}
71	\label{sec:intro}
72	
73	Cardiovascular disease (CVD) remains the leading cause of death worldwide, and ischemic heart disease alone accounted for approximately 9.0 million deaths in 2021~\cite{GBD2021}. Atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major mechanism underlying acute coronary events. Rupture of the fibrous cap exposes thrombogenic plaque contents to the bloodstream and can precipitate coronary thrombosis and myocardial infarction~\cite{bentzon2014, costopoulos2017}. From a mechanical perspective, rupture occurs when the local stress acting on the fibrous cap exceeds its mechanical strength~\cite{costopoulos2017}. The mechanical loading of the fibrous cap is therefore central to plaque stability and rupture risk.
74	
75	Advances in coronary computed tomography angiography and intravascular imaging have enabled increasingly detailed \textit{in vivo} characterization of plaque geometry and composition~\cite{lu2021highrisk, williams2020lap, milzi2021}. These developments have also supported image-based computational models that estimate local mechanical quantities not directly available from imaging alone. Among these quantities, plaque structural stress (PSS), the mechanical stress developed within the plaque tissue (particularly the fibrous cap) under physiological loading, is widely used to characterize the mechanical loading associated with plaque rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. The local stress field depends on plaque geometry, tissue material properties, and the applied physiological loading~\cite{huang2001, costopoulos2017, stefanati2024}. Clinical imaging studies have further associated elevated PSS with plaque composition, phenotype, and rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017, milzi2021}. PSS is commonly evaluated at or near systole to represent peak plaque loading~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. Other studies have examined the pulsatile variation in structural stress ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$), motivated by repeated cyclic loading and potential fatigue-related damage accumulation~\cite{bank2000, costopoulos2017}. The present study therefore considers both PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ as complementary stress-based measures of plaque vulnerability.
76	
77	However, estimating a vulnerability index for a given lesion does not identify which underlying inputs drive variation in that index across the plausible parameter space. This distinction is important because plaque morphology, tissue properties, and hemodynamic conditions all vary between patients. Moreover, these quantities cannot be measured or prescribed with equal accuracy in patient-specific models. Identifying the dominant contributors can therefore help prioritize measurement, calibration, and model-refinement efforts. Global sensitivity analysis (GSA) provides a systematic framework for this purpose by quantifying how uncertain inputs contribute to output variability over their prescribed ranges~\cite{saltelli2008}. Variance-based GSA can also capture interaction effects that are not resolved when factors are examined only in isolation~\cite{saltelli2008}. Such interactions are particularly relevant to plaque mechanics because the effect of a geometric feature may depend on the surrounding material and hemodynamic state.
78	

