135:For each sample, the locations of the maximum PSS and maximum $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ were localized axially and circumferentially (Fig.~\ref{fig:ruploc}). Axially, each location was classified as proximal, mid, or distal using the lumen area ratio $LA(z) = A_\mathrm{lumen}(z)/A_\mathrm{normal}$, with the mid region bounded by the two locations where $LA = 0.3$, following Chung et al.~\cite{chung2017_LA}. Circumferentially, the angle $\theta_\mathrm{rupture}$ from the reference $y$-axis to the rupture node, measured on the rupture plane, was normalized by the lipid arc angle $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$ on the same cross-section:
145:Three of the five features were represented directly by baseline input variables ($E_\mathrm{lipid}$, $f_\mathrm{calc}$, and $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$). The remaining two, plaque burden ($PB$) and functional gradient ($\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$), were not input variables but were instead derived from the geometry and simulation results. Plaque burden was evaluated at the minimum-lumen-area (MLA) cross-section, defined as the plane at which the lumen diameter was smallest. It was computed in the conventional cross-sectional form as $PB = (A_\mathrm{vessel} - A_\mathrm{lumen})/A_\mathrm{vessel} \times 100\%$, where $A_\mathrm{vessel}$ and $A_\mathrm{lumen}$ are the vessel-wall-bounded and luminal areas on that plane~\cite{mintz2001ivus}. $\Delta\mathrm{FFR}$ was computed as $FFR_\mathrm{prox} - FFR_\mathrm{dist} = 1 - P_d/P_\mathrm{in}$, with the distal pressure $P_d$ taken as the cross-sectional mean $3\,\mathrm{cm}$ downstream of the lesion~\cite{lee2019emerald, cami2018ffrct}.
362:    \caption{Rupture-location classification. \textbf{(A)} Axial classification into proximal, mid, and distal regions using the lumen area ratio $LA(z) = A_\mathrm{lumen}/A_\mathrm{normal}$, with the mid region bounded by the two locations where $LA = 0.3$. \textbf{(B)} Circumferential classification on the rupture plane, where the angle $\theta_\mathrm{rupture}$ is normalized by the lipid arc angle $\theta_\mathrm{lipid}$ to define center ($I_\theta < 0.5$) versus shoulder ($I_\theta \geq 0.5$) locations.}

