53	\begin{abstract}
54	Coronary atherosclerotic plaque rupture is a major cause of acute coronary events, and its risk is governed jointly by plaque morphology, tissue material properties, and hemodynamic loading. Which of these factor groups most strongly governs rupture-risk variability remains poorly characterized, partly because pulsatile fluid--structure interaction (FSI) is computationally expensive. Here, a cost-effective snapshot-based FSI formulation was used as the forward model to generate large input--output datasets for low-attenuation (LAP) and calcified (CP) plaque phenotypes, sampled by Latin hypercube design over morphological, material, and hemodynamic parameters. Two stress-based vulnerability indices, the peak plaque structural stress (PSS) and its pulsatile amplitude ($\Delta$PSS), were evaluated and first checked for directional consistency with five clinically recognized high-risk plaque features. Gaussian process regression surrogates were then trained for each index, and Sobol variance-based global sensitivity analysis was performed at the individual-variable and factor-group levels. $\Delta$PSS was consistent with all five high-risk features, whereas PSS was consistent with three, indicating that the pulsatile stress amplitude is the more clinically consistent index. The surrogates were accurate ($R^2 = 0.94$--$0.97$). Across both phenotypes and indices, the vessel-wall modulus, fibrous-cap modulus, and systolic pressure were the dominant contributors, and the material group showed the largest total-order contribution ($S_T = 0.596$--$0.708$), followed by the hemodynamic group, whereas morphology contributed mainly through interactions. These results indicate that coronary plaque rupture-risk variability is governed primarily by material and hemodynamic factors rather than by morphology alone, providing a basis for prioritizing the quantities that warrant accurate patient-specific characterization.
55	\end{abstract}
56	
57	\begin{highlights}
58	\item Morphological, material, and hemodynamic factors were systematically varied in fluid--structure interaction simulations.
59	\item Plaque structural stress (PSS) and pulsatile stress variation ($\Delta$PSS) were evaluated as complementary rupture-risk metrics.
60	\item Pulsatile $\Delta$PSS showed greater consistency with clinical plaque vulnerability than peak systolic PSS.
61	\item Sobol sensitivity analysis identified material properties as the dominant determinants of plaque rupture risk.
62	\end{highlights}
