71	\section{Introduction}
72	\label{sec:intro}
73	
74	Cardiovascular disease (CVD) remains the leading cause of death worldwide, with ischemic heart disease alone accounting for approximately 9.0 million deaths in 2021~\cite{GBD2021}. Among the major mechanisms underlying acute coronary events, atherosclerotic plaque rupture plays a central role. Failure of the fibrous cap exposes thrombogenic plaque contents to the bloodstream, thereby triggering coronary thrombosis and potentially myocardial infarction~\cite{bentzon2014, costopoulos2017}.
75	From a mechanical perspective, rupture occurs when the local stress acting on the fibrous cap exceeds its mechanical strength~\cite{costopoulos2017}. The mechanical loading of the fibrous cap is therefore central to plaque stability and rupture risk.
76	
77	Advances in coronary computed tomography angiography and intravascular imaging have enabled increasingly detailed \textit{in vivo} characterization of plaque geometry and composition~\cite{lu2021highrisk, williams2020lap, milzi2021}. These developments have also supported image-based computational models that estimate local mechanical quantities not directly available from imaging alone. Among these quantities, plaque structural stress (PSS), the mechanical stress developed within the plaque tissue (particularly the fibrous cap) under physiological loading, is widely used to characterize the mechanical loading associated with plaque rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. The local stress field depends on plaque geometry, tissue material properties, and the applied physiological loading~\cite{huang2001, costopoulos2017, stefanati2024}. Clinical imaging studies have further associated elevated PSS with plaque composition, phenotype, and rupture~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017, milzi2021}. PSS is commonly evaluated at or near systole to represent peak plaque loading~\cite{teng2014, costopoulos2017}. Other studies have examined the pulsatile variation in structural stress ($\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$), motivated by repeated cyclic loading and potential fatigue-related damage accumulation~\cite{bank2000, costopoulos2017}. The present study therefore considers both PSS and $\Delta\mathrm{PSS}$ as complementary stress-based measures of plaque vulnerability.
